# React & Next.js Architecture

## Server vs Client

- Default to Server Components. Add `'use client'` only when the file needs state, effects, event handlers, or browser APIs.
- Keep Client Components small and declarative. Fetch data and handle secrets on the server (Route Handlers, Server Actions, server utils).
- Never import server-only modules (`prisma`, Redis, Clerk backend secrets) into Client Components.

## SOLID & SRP

- UI components stay presentational. Extract business logic, mutations, and fetching into hooks, Server Actions, or `src/lib/*`.
- One reason to change per module. Do not grow page files into god components.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — fetch + secret + UI in a client page
'use client';
export default function Page() {
  const [data, setData] = useState<any>(null);
  useEffect(() => { fetch('/api/me').then(r => r.json()).then(setData); }, []);
  return <pre>{JSON.stringify(data)}</pre>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — server fetch, typed, thin UI
export default async function Page() {
  const me = await getCurrentUser();
  return <ProfileView user={me} />;
}
```

## Strict TypeScript

- No `any`. Use interfaces, generics, `Omit` / `Pick`, and discriminated unions.
- Explicitly type component props, hook returns, and Route Handler inputs/outputs.
- Prefer existing types in `src/lib/duo-types.ts` and Prisma models over duplicating shapes.
